    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0236" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A3/1859: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-12-24T11:00:00+00:00">December 24,
                            1936—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 24—8:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>624. Supplementing statement of First Lord in Parliament on December 17 (see my
                    618, December 16, 8 p.m.) I received a Foreign Office note yesterday<anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d139-f1"/> which states the British Government consider the
                    requirements of national security necessitates the retention of five “C”
                    cruisers. “The retention of these vessels will cause the tonnage for the cruiser
                    category permitted under the London Naval Treaty, 1930 (339,000 tons), to be
                    exceeded by 20,270 tons, and His Majesty’s Government accordingly propose to
                    have recourse to article 21 of that treaty in order to keep this excess tonnage”
                    and concludes “I have the honor to add that His Majesty’s Government undertake
                    that (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) vessels shall be retained for a maximum of
                    5 years’ peace service, and (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) they will be used
                    not as cruisers but as antiaircraft ships, which will involve the substitution
                    of a lighter armament for the existing 6-inch gun armament.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d139-f1" n="44" xml:id="d139-f1-content">
                <p>The copy of the British note transmitted to the Department by the Ambassador in
                    his despatch No. 2731, December 23 (not printed) was dated December 22.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
